# Files that run inside a Linux container must keep LF endings regardless of the
# machine they are checked out on.
#
# Windows sets core.autocrlf=true by default, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. A shell script that arrives with CRLF has a shebang of
# "#!/bin/sh\r", and the kernel looks for an interpreter literally named
# "/bin/sh\r". The container then fails with "no such file or directory" naming
# the entrypoint that plainly exists - one of the more misleading errors in
# Docker, and worth a rule to never meet.

*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
